+/* ======================================================================= */
+/* Environment-variable activity tracer.                                   */
+/*                                                                         */
+/* Emits one newline-delimited record to a file or file descriptor every  */
+/* time a shell variable is created, modified (even to an identical        */
+/* value), unset, or has its export attribute toggled, and for temporary-  */
+/* environment assignments of the form `FOO=bar cmd'.  This is compiled    */
+/* unconditionally and is inert unless activated at run time.              */
+/*                                                                         */
+/* Activation and runtime control (the variables need only be SET -- the   */
+/* tracer reads bash's own variable table, so no export is required):      */
+/*   BASH_ENVTRACE_FD=<n>     write to already-open descriptor <n>         */
+/*   BASH_ENVTRACE_FILE=<path> open <path> O_WRONLY|O_CREAT|O_APPEND       */
+/* Both are read lazily at the first traced mutation (BASH_ENVTRACE_FD     */
+/* wins if both are set), and re-read whenever the script itself assigns   */
+/* or unsets either variable:                                              */
+/*   - assigning one replaces the current sink ("most recent wins"); an    */
+/*     empty or unusable value leaves tracing disabled;                    */
+/*   - `unset' of the variable that configured the active sink stops       */
+/*     tracing.  Control-variable mutations are themselves never recorded  */
+/*     (see below), so the stream carries no window-marker records.        */
+/* This makes windowed capture of one section of a script trivial:         */
+/*   BASH_ENVTRACE_FILE=$t; ...critical section...; unset BASH_ENVTRACE_FILE */
+/* If neither variable is ever set the tracer stays disabled with no       */
+/* measurable overhead beyond one integer test per mutation.               */
+/*                                                                         */
+/* Records are suppressed until shell_initialized != 0, which excludes the */
+/* initial import of the inherited environment and any startup files, so   */
+/* the stream reflects only the activity of the script/-c body itself.     */
+/*                                                                         */
+/* Exactly ONE process ever writes a given trace: forked subshells          */
+/* ( (...), $(...), cmd &, process substitution ) inherit the tracer state  */
+/* but are suppressed at emit time via bash's subshell globals (plus an     */
+/* owner-pid guard), and the control variables are excluded from the        */
+/* export environment built for children even if the user exports them,     */
+/* so an exec'd patched bash can never activate on an inherited setting.    */
+/* Subshell mutations cannot reach this shell's environment anyway; the     */
+/* stream records exactly the mutations that can.                           */
+/*                                                                          */
+/* Record format (fields separated by 0x1f, record terminated by 0x0a):   */
+/*   <sec>.<usec> <op> <exported> <name> <old> <operand>                    */
+/* <op> is one of:                                                          */
+/*   set      entry appears in the environment: creation of an exported     */
+/*            variable, or `export NAME' on a variable built up while       */
+/*            unexported (its accumulated value is reported here -- the     */
+/*            variable table itself is the buffer, so unexported            */
+/*            modifications are never reported piecemeal)                   */
+/*   reset    member reassigned a byte-identical value                      */
+/*   prepend  member grew at the front                                      */
+/*   append   member grew at the end                                        */
+/*   updated  member changed any other way (shrink, rewrite, both ends)     */
+/*   unset    entry removed: `unset' of a member, or `export -n'            */
+/*   tempenv  `FOO=bar cmd' overlay (child-only, parent env untouched)      */
+/* There is no `export' op: the export builtin's effect is reported as the  */
+/* `set' (or `unset' for -n) it amounts to; a redundant re-export changes   */
+/* nothing and reports nothing.                                             */
+/*                                                                          */
+/* <old> is the full prior value.  <operand> is the argument to <op>: the   */
+/* full assigned value for set/reset/updated/tempenv, ONLY the added text   */
+/* for prepend/append (so a replaying shell can apply the delta to its own  */
+/* current value, e.g. append `:/usr/sbin' to its own PATH), and `@' for    */
+/* unset.                                                                   */
+/* An empty old value classifies as `append' (it is trivially a prefix of   */
+/* the new value); when the old value is both a proper prefix and a proper  */
+/* suffix of the new one, prefix wins and the op is `append'.               */
+/*                                                                          */
+/* <exported> is two digits, the PRE- and POST-operation export state, so   */
+/* every record is invertible: `01' = newly exported, `11' = was and still  */
+/* is, `10' = removed from the environment.  Records are emitted ONLY for   */
+/* mutations visible to child-process environments (pre or post is 1), so   */
+/* `00' never appears; plain unexported shell-variable activity -- and any  */
+/* mutation of the control variables themselves -- is not recorded.  For    */
+/* `unset' the post digit is always 0; for `tempenv' it is `01' (an entry  */
+/* appears in the child's environment; the parent is untouched).  The      */
+/* digits report actual environment membership -- export attribute AND a   */
+/* value -- so an invisible `declare -x' placeholder is not a member.      */
+/* <exported> is two digits: pre- and post-operation state (see below).    */
+/* <name> is escaped.  <old>/<new> are either the single byte '@' meaning  */
+/* "did not exist", or ':' followed by the escaped value (which may be     */
+/* empty).  Escaping renders \\, 0x0a, 0x0d, 0x1f and bytes < 0x20 as      */
+/* \xHH / \n / \r / \\, so every record occupies exactly one line and no   */
+/* value byte can be confused with a delimiter.                            */
+/* ======================================================================= */
